1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,178
There was a time when Sunday in

2
00:00:02,178 --> 00:00:05,690
America felt completely different from every other

3
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:06,815
day of the week.

4
00:00:06,995 --> 00:00:08,469
The stores were closed.

5
00:00:08,649 --> 00:00:10,007
The streets were quiet.

6
00:00:10,187 --> 00:00:12,115
Church bells rang across town.

7
00:00:12,295 --> 00:00:14,975
Families dressed in their best clothes,

8
00:00:14,975 --> 00:00:16,693
sat down to a big dinner,

9
00:00:16,693 --> 00:00:18,962
and spent the afternoon together,

10
00:00:18,962 --> 00:00:20,886
doing almost nothing at all.

11
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:23,094
If you grew up in the fifties,

12
00:00:23,094 --> 00:00:24,851
you remember that feeling.

13
00:00:25,031 --> 00:00:26,830
Sunday was a day apart.

14
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:30,465
Today, Sunday is just another day for

15
00:00:30,465 --> 00:00:31,441
many of us.

16
00:00:31,621 --> 00:00:33,193
We shop, run errands,

17
00:00:33,193 --> 00:00:35,662
answer emails and watch the game.

18
00:00:35,842 --> 00:00:38,069
But in the nineteen fifties,

19
00:00:38,069 --> 00:00:40,137
Sunday had its own rhythm,

20
00:00:40,137 --> 00:00:42,761
its own rules and its own sounds.

21
00:00:42,941 --> 00:00:44,945
And for millions of Americans,

22
00:00:44,945 --> 00:00:46,682
it was the best day of the

23
00:00:46,682 --> 00:00:47,016
week.

24
00:00:47,366 --> 00:00:49,799
So let's spend a Sunday in nineteen

25
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:51,466
fifty-six, hour by hour,

26
00:00:51,466 --> 00:00:53,759
from the first church bell in the

27
00:00:53,759 --> 00:00:56,400
morning to the last television show at

28
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:56,817
night.

29
00:00:56,997 --> 00:00:58,111
If you were there,

30
00:00:58,111 --> 00:01:00,214
I think you'll smell the pot roast

31
00:01:00,214 --> 00:01:01,328
before we're done.

32
00:01:01,678 --> 00:01:04,286
Sunday morning started a little later than

33
00:01:04,286 --> 00:01:05,776
a weekday, but not much.

34
00:01:05,956 --> 00:01:07,319
Mothers were up early,

35
00:01:07,319 --> 00:01:09,551
putting the roast in the oven before

36
00:01:09,551 --> 00:01:09,984
church.

37
00:01:10,164 --> 00:01:12,959
Fathers read the thick Sunday newspaper,

38
00:01:12,959 --> 00:01:15,124
with its sections and sections,

39
00:01:15,124 --> 00:01:18,197
and the colored comics that children grabbed

40
00:01:18,197 --> 00:01:18,617
first.

41
00:01:18,797 --> 00:01:20,666
Dick Tracy, Blondie,

42
00:01:20,666 --> 00:01:24,311
Li'l Abner, Prince Valiant and Peanuts.

43
00:01:24,661 --> 00:01:27,483
Breakfast on Sunday was often special too.

44
00:01:27,663 --> 00:01:31,374
Pancakes, waffles, or eggs with bacon and

45
00:01:31,374 --> 00:01:32,098
sausage.

46
00:01:32,278 --> 00:01:33,282
In some homes,

47
00:01:33,282 --> 00:01:35,434
Dad made breakfast on Sundays,

48
00:01:35,434 --> 00:01:37,372
the only day of the week he

49
00:01:37,372 --> 00:01:37,874
cooked.

50
00:01:38,054 --> 00:01:40,289
In others, families who were going to

51
00:01:40,289 --> 00:01:42,827
take communion skipped breakfast entirely,

52
00:01:42,827 --> 00:01:45,667
and the children's stomachs growled all through

53
00:01:45,667 --> 00:01:46,332
the sermon.

54
00:01:46,681 --> 00:01:49,070
Then came the scramble to get everyone

55
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:49,572
dressed.

56
00:01:49,752 --> 00:01:51,630
Sunday clothes were special,

57
00:01:51,630 --> 00:01:53,038
kept just for church.

58
00:01:53,218 --> 00:01:54,865
Boys wore white shirts,

59
00:01:54,865 --> 00:01:57,874
clip-on ties and stiff shoes that pinched.

60
00:01:58,054 --> 00:01:59,998
Girls wore starched dresses,

61
00:01:59,998 --> 00:02:02,983
white socks, patent leather shoes and maybe

62
00:02:02,983 --> 00:02:05,066
white gloves and a little hat.

63
00:02:05,246 --> 00:02:07,101
Their mothers wore hats too,

64
00:02:07,101 --> 00:02:09,286
and gloves, and their best dress.

65
00:02:09,637 --> 00:02:12,495
Fathers wore suits and polished shoes,

66
00:02:12,495 --> 00:02:14,826
often shined on Saturday night.

67
00:02:15,006 --> 00:02:17,317
Hair was combed and slicked down.

68
00:02:17,497 --> 00:02:19,064
Faces were scrubbed.

69
00:02:19,244 --> 00:02:21,701
And mothers inspected everyone at the door

70
00:02:21,701 --> 00:02:23,632
before they could get in the car.

71
00:02:23,812 --> 00:02:25,403
Did you wash behind your ears?

72
00:02:25,583 --> 00:02:26,988
Where's your other glove?

73
00:02:27,168 --> 00:02:29,107
Don't get dirty before we get there.

74
00:02:29,457 --> 00:02:31,478
Getting to church was part of the

75
00:02:31,478 --> 00:02:31,906
ritual.

76
00:02:32,086 --> 00:02:33,128
In small towns,

77
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:36,602
families walked, greeting neighbors along the way.

78
00:02:36,782 --> 00:02:37,821
In the suburbs,

79
00:02:37,821 --> 00:02:40,451
they drove, and the church parking lot

80
00:02:40,451 --> 00:02:42,527
filled up with big shiny cars,

81
00:02:42,527 --> 00:02:44,881
many of them washed the day before

82
00:02:44,881 --> 00:02:45,850
just for this.

83
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:48,824
Late arrivals tiptoed in during the first

84
00:02:48,824 --> 00:02:51,278
hymn, while everyone turned to look.

85
00:02:51,628 --> 00:02:53,439
In the nineteen fifties,

86
00:02:53,439 --> 00:02:56,231
church attendance in America was at a

87
00:02:56,231 --> 00:02:57,061
high point.

88
00:02:57,241 --> 00:02:59,791
Surveys at the time found that about

89
00:02:59,791 --> 00:03:03,049
half of all American adults attended religious

90
00:03:03,049 --> 00:03:04,962
services in a typical week.

91
00:03:05,142 --> 00:03:07,887
New churches were being built across the

92
00:03:07,887 --> 00:03:10,563
country, especially in the new suburbs.

93
00:03:10,743 --> 00:03:14,738
Sunday school classrooms overflowed with baby boom

94
00:03:14,738 --> 00:03:15,457
children.

95
00:03:15,808 --> 00:03:18,166
Families sat in the same pew every

96
00:03:18,166 --> 00:03:18,513
week.

97
00:03:18,693 --> 00:03:21,462
Children were expected to sit still and

98
00:03:21,462 --> 00:03:24,161
be quiet, which was almost impossible.

99
00:03:24,341 --> 00:03:26,821
Mothers kept them in line with a

100
00:03:26,821 --> 00:03:27,906
look, a pinch,

101
00:03:27,906 --> 00:03:30,308
or a peppermint from her purse.

102
00:03:30,488 --> 00:03:33,437
Fathers sometimes dozed during the sermon.

103
00:03:33,617 --> 00:03:35,999
And everybody stood up and sat down

104
00:03:35,999 --> 00:03:37,565
at all the right times.

105
00:03:37,915 --> 00:03:40,229
Children went to Sunday school,

106
00:03:40,229 --> 00:03:43,365
where they learned Bible stories from felt

107
00:03:43,365 --> 00:03:45,231
boards, colored pictures,

108
00:03:45,231 --> 00:03:47,620
memorized verses and sang songs.

109
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:49,043
Jesus Loves Me.

110
00:03:49,223 --> 00:03:51,022
This Little Light of Mine.

111
00:03:51,202 --> 00:03:53,686
They got little cards with pictures for

112
00:03:53,686 --> 00:03:56,425
perfect attendance, and pins for every year

113
00:03:56,425 --> 00:03:58,017
without missing a Sunday.

114
00:03:58,368 --> 00:03:59,993
And there was the collection plate.

115
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:02,171
Children were given a coin,

116
00:04:02,171 --> 00:04:03,578
a nickel or a dime,

117
00:04:03,578 --> 00:04:05,724
to put in when the plate came

118
00:04:05,724 --> 00:04:06,686
down the pew.

119
00:04:06,866 --> 00:04:08,754
Some kept it in their shoe,

120
00:04:08,754 --> 00:04:10,641
or tied it in the corner of

121
00:04:10,641 --> 00:04:13,507
a handkerchief, so they wouldn't lose it.

122
00:04:13,687 --> 00:04:16,293
Many children learned their first lessons about

123
00:04:16,293 --> 00:04:17,901
giving from that little coin.

124
00:04:18,252 --> 00:04:19,497
After the service,

125
00:04:19,497 --> 00:04:22,748
the congregation gathered outside on the church

126
00:04:22,748 --> 00:04:23,163
steps.

127
00:04:23,343 --> 00:04:25,496
Men shook hands with the minister and

128
00:04:25,496 --> 00:04:27,940
talked about the weather and the ballgame.

129
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:30,984
Women admired each other's hats and new

130
00:04:30,984 --> 00:04:31,499
babies.

131
00:04:31,679 --> 00:04:33,861
Children ran around on the lawn in

132
00:04:33,861 --> 00:04:35,081
their good clothes,

133
00:04:35,081 --> 00:04:37,391
which was exactly what they had been

134
00:04:37,391 --> 00:04:38,354
told not to do.

135
00:04:38,705 --> 00:04:41,112
Faith was not the same for everyone,

136
00:04:41,112 --> 00:04:41,781
of course.

137
00:04:41,961 --> 00:04:45,340
Jewish families observed the Sabbath on Saturday.

138
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,702
Catholic families went to Mass,

139
00:04:47,702 --> 00:04:49,602
often early in the morning,

140
00:04:49,602 --> 00:04:52,207
with the service still said in Latin.

141
00:04:52,387 --> 00:04:54,380
Many towns had a Methodist,

142
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:57,038
a Baptist, a Lutheran and a Catholic

143
00:04:57,038 --> 00:04:59,549
church within a few blocks of each

144
00:04:59,549 --> 00:04:59,992
other.

145
00:05:00,172 --> 00:05:01,404
On Sunday morning,

146
00:05:01,404 --> 00:05:03,800
their bells rang at nearly the same

147
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:04,142
time.

148
00:05:04,493 --> 00:05:06,318
Then home for Sunday dinner,

149
00:05:06,318 --> 00:05:08,208
the biggest meal of the week.

150
00:05:08,388 --> 00:05:09,577
In many families,

151
00:05:09,577 --> 00:05:12,165
it was served in the early afternoon,

152
00:05:12,165 --> 00:05:13,984
around one or two o'clock.

153
00:05:14,164 --> 00:05:16,429
The dining room table was set with

154
00:05:16,429 --> 00:05:17,761
the good tablecloth,

155
00:05:17,761 --> 00:05:19,760
the good china and the silver.

156
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:21,572
It was the one meal of the

157
00:05:21,572 --> 00:05:23,644
week that was eaten in the dining

158
00:05:23,644 --> 00:05:25,025
room, not the kitchen.

159
00:05:25,375 --> 00:05:27,360
The menu was usually the same.

160
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:30,533
Pot roast with carrots and potatoes,

161
00:05:30,533 --> 00:05:32,112
or a roast chicken,

162
00:05:32,112 --> 00:05:33,526
or fried chicken,

163
00:05:33,526 --> 00:05:34,773
or a baked ham.

164
00:05:34,953 --> 00:05:37,101
Mashed potatoes and gravy.

165
00:05:37,281 --> 00:05:39,278
Green beans or peas.

166
00:05:39,458 --> 00:05:41,338
Homemade rolls with butter.

167
00:05:41,518 --> 00:05:42,819
A Jell-O salad.

168
00:05:42,999 --> 00:05:44,635
And a pie or a cake for

169
00:05:44,635 --> 00:05:45,205
dessert.

170
00:05:45,385 --> 00:05:48,183
Grandmothers often brought their specialty,

171
00:05:48,183 --> 00:05:50,460
and nobody else was allowed to make

172
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:50,656
it.

173
00:05:51,006 --> 00:05:53,371
Many families had grandparents,

174
00:05:53,371 --> 00:05:56,729
aunts, uncles and cousins over every Sunday.

175
00:05:56,909 --> 00:05:59,162
Or they drove to Grandma's house.

176
00:05:59,342 --> 00:06:01,784
The children sat at a separate kids'

177
00:06:01,784 --> 00:06:03,684
table, often in the kitchen.

178
00:06:03,864 --> 00:06:06,551
The grown-ups talked about family news,

179
00:06:06,551 --> 00:06:09,100
politics and the price of everything.

180
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:12,212
And somebody always said grace before anyone

181
00:06:12,212 --> 00:06:12,879
could eat.

182
00:06:13,229 --> 00:06:15,857
After dinner, the women usually cleared the

183
00:06:15,857 --> 00:06:17,569
table and washed the dishes,

184
00:06:17,569 --> 00:06:18,975
talking in the kitchen.

185
00:06:19,155 --> 00:06:21,311
The men retired to the living room,

186
00:06:21,311 --> 00:06:22,542
loosened their ties,

187
00:06:22,542 --> 00:06:24,020
and settled into chairs.

188
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:25,805
Within half an hour,

189
00:06:25,805 --> 00:06:27,811
many of them were asleep.

190
00:06:28,161 --> 00:06:30,909
Children who finished their dinner asked to

191
00:06:30,909 --> 00:06:32,698
be excused, and ran outside,

192
00:06:32,698 --> 00:06:34,488
still in their good clothes.

193
00:06:34,668 --> 00:06:36,742
Older kids might be allowed to go

194
00:06:36,742 --> 00:06:39,067
to the afternoon matinee at the movie

195
00:06:39,067 --> 00:06:41,833
theater, if their parents approved of Sunday

196
00:06:41,833 --> 00:06:42,273
movies.

197
00:06:42,453 --> 00:06:44,688
Others were stuck in the living room

198
00:06:44,688 --> 00:06:45,868
with the grown-ups,

199
00:06:45,868 --> 00:06:48,166
listening to stories they had heard a

200
00:06:48,166 --> 00:06:49,035
hundred times.

201
00:06:49,386 --> 00:06:51,800
Outside, the town was quiet.

202
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:54,568
In much of America in the nineteen

203
00:06:54,568 --> 00:06:57,460
fifties, stores were closed on Sunday.

204
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:59,708
In many states and towns,

205
00:06:59,708 --> 00:07:02,850
so-called blue laws made it illegal to

206
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:05,082
sell many things on Sunday.

207
00:07:05,262 --> 00:07:08,861
Car dealers, department stores and hardware stores

208
00:07:08,861 --> 00:07:09,581
were dark.

209
00:07:09,761 --> 00:07:10,873
In some places,

210
00:07:10,873 --> 00:07:13,540
even buying a hammer was against the

211
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:13,836
law.

212
00:07:14,187 --> 00:07:16,195
A few places stayed open.

213
00:07:16,375 --> 00:07:18,615
The drugstore, for medicine,

214
00:07:18,615 --> 00:07:20,775
might open for a few hours.

215
00:07:20,955 --> 00:07:22,232
Gas stations.

216
00:07:22,412 --> 00:07:23,689
Some restaurants.

217
00:07:23,869 --> 00:07:26,717
Movie theaters opened in the afternoon in

218
00:07:26,717 --> 00:07:27,550
many places.

219
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:29,089
But in many states,

220
00:07:29,089 --> 00:07:30,949
liquor stores were closed,

221
00:07:30,949 --> 00:07:32,165
and so were bars.

222
00:07:32,345 --> 00:07:35,107
Main Street on a Sunday afternoon could

223
00:07:35,107 --> 00:07:36,594
look almost deserted.

224
00:07:36,944 --> 00:07:39,745
Blue laws went back to colonial times,

225
00:07:39,745 --> 00:07:41,956
and many of them stayed on the

226
00:07:41,956 --> 00:07:43,283
books for decades.

227
00:07:43,463 --> 00:07:45,114
In nineteen sixty-one,

228
00:07:45,114 --> 00:07:47,667
the Supreme Court upheld them in a

229
00:07:47,667 --> 00:07:50,371
case called McGowan versus Maryland.

230
00:07:50,551 --> 00:07:53,557
It wasn't until the seventies and eighties

231
00:07:53,557 --> 00:07:56,348
that most states began to allow regular

232
00:07:56,348 --> 00:07:57,493
Sunday shopping.

233
00:07:57,844 --> 00:08:01,009
Even radio and television felt different on

234
00:08:01,009 --> 00:08:01,524
Sunday.

235
00:08:01,704 --> 00:08:04,808
Mornings were full of religious programs,

236
00:08:04,808 --> 00:08:06,928
preachers and gospel choirs.

237
00:08:07,108 --> 00:08:09,989
Shows like The Lutheran Hour and Bishop

238
00:08:09,989 --> 00:08:12,944
Fulton Sheen's Life Is Worth Living drew

239
00:08:12,944 --> 00:08:15,456
millions of listeners and viewers.

240
00:08:15,636 --> 00:08:17,906
Sheen was so popular that he won

241
00:08:17,906 --> 00:08:18,899
an Emmy Award,

242
00:08:18,899 --> 00:08:21,665
and competed in the ratings with Milton

243
00:08:21,665 --> 00:08:22,091
Berle.

244
00:08:22,441 --> 00:08:24,953
So what did families do on Sunday

245
00:08:24,953 --> 00:08:25,715
afternoon?

246
00:08:25,895 --> 00:08:28,042
Many went for a Sunday drive.

247
00:08:28,222 --> 00:08:30,880
The whole family piled into the car,

248
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:33,168
with no particular destination.

249
00:08:33,348 --> 00:08:35,653
They drove into the countryside,

250
00:08:35,653 --> 00:08:37,741
past farms, lakes and fields,

251
00:08:37,741 --> 00:08:39,397
looking at the scenery.

252
00:08:39,577 --> 00:08:42,055
They might stop for an ice cream

253
00:08:42,055 --> 00:08:43,992
cone at a roadside stand,

254
00:08:43,992 --> 00:08:45,696
then drive home again.

255
00:08:46,046 --> 00:08:47,427
Others went visiting.

256
00:08:47,607 --> 00:08:50,560
Sunday afternoon was the time to call

257
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:53,911
on relatives, neighbors and older friends.

258
00:08:54,091 --> 00:08:56,404
You dropped in without calling first,

259
00:08:56,404 --> 00:08:58,155
and you were always welcome.

260
00:08:58,335 --> 00:08:59,797
There would be coffee,

261
00:08:59,797 --> 00:09:01,060
a plate of cookies,

262
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:03,386
and conversation on the porch or in

263
00:09:03,386 --> 00:09:04,117
the parlor.

264
00:09:04,467 --> 00:09:06,389
Children played outside,

265
00:09:06,389 --> 00:09:09,273
but often with special Sunday rules.

266
00:09:09,453 --> 00:09:11,635
In many religious homes,

267
00:09:11,635 --> 00:09:12,999
no rough games,

268
00:09:12,999 --> 00:09:14,454
no card playing,

269
00:09:14,454 --> 00:09:16,454
and no work on Sunday.

270
00:09:16,634 --> 00:09:19,510
Some families didn't even allow sewing or

271
00:09:19,510 --> 00:09:21,684
mowing the lawn on the Sabbath.

272
00:09:21,864 --> 00:09:24,842
Kids found ways to have fun anyway,

273
00:09:24,842 --> 00:09:26,289
with board games,

274
00:09:26,289 --> 00:09:28,331
reading, or quiet walks.

275
00:09:28,681 --> 00:09:29,574
In the fall,

276
00:09:29,574 --> 00:09:32,926
Sunday afternoon meant football on the radio,

277
00:09:32,926 --> 00:09:35,310
and increasingly, on television.

278
00:09:35,490 --> 00:09:38,457
Professional football was growing fast in the

279
00:09:38,457 --> 00:09:38,984
fifties.

280
00:09:39,164 --> 00:09:43,287
The famous nineteen fifty-eight championship game between

281
00:09:43,287 --> 00:09:45,890
the Baltimore Colts and the New York

282
00:09:45,890 --> 00:09:48,277
Giants, which went into overtime,

283
00:09:48,277 --> 00:09:51,097
was watched by millions and helped make

284
00:09:51,097 --> 00:09:53,050
the NFL a national passion.

285
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,976
In summer, there might be a picnic

286
00:09:55,976 --> 00:09:56,885
in the park,

287
00:09:56,885 --> 00:09:58,098
a church social,

288
00:09:58,098 --> 00:10:01,053
or a baseball doubleheader at the local

289
00:10:01,053 --> 00:10:01,736
ballpark.

290
00:10:01,916 --> 00:10:04,284
Families packed fried chicken,

291
00:10:04,284 --> 00:10:06,969
potato salad and deviled eggs in a

292
00:10:06,969 --> 00:10:09,970
basket, spread a blanket under a tree,

293
00:10:09,970 --> 00:10:13,049
and spent the whole afternoon outdoors.

294
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:14,596
And in the evening,

295
00:10:14,596 --> 00:10:16,925
many families went back to church for

296
00:10:16,925 --> 00:10:19,065
an evening service or youth group.

297
00:10:19,245 --> 00:10:21,909
Teenagers went to youth fellowship meetings,

298
00:10:21,909 --> 00:10:24,149
which were often as much about seeing

299
00:10:24,149 --> 00:10:25,724
friends as about religion.

300
00:10:25,904 --> 00:10:27,379
There were hymn sings,

301
00:10:27,379 --> 00:10:28,721
potluck suppers and,

302
00:10:28,721 --> 00:10:31,203
in summer, revival meetings in a tent

303
00:10:31,203 --> 00:10:32,544
at the edge of town.

304
00:10:32,895 --> 00:10:34,672
Sunday supper was light,

305
00:10:34,672 --> 00:10:36,819
since dinner had been so big.

306
00:10:36,999 --> 00:10:39,352
Leftovers, cold cuts,

307
00:10:39,352 --> 00:10:42,490
sandwiches, soup or popcorn.

308
00:10:42,670 --> 00:10:45,700
Sometimes just cereal or toast.

309
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:47,607
Mothers needed a rest too,

310
00:10:47,607 --> 00:10:49,998
and Sunday evening was often the one

311
00:10:49,998 --> 00:10:52,521
meal of the week nobody really cooked.

312
00:10:52,872 --> 00:10:55,351
Then came Sunday night television,

313
00:10:55,351 --> 00:10:57,393
and it was the best night of

314
00:10:57,393 --> 00:10:58,050
the week.

315
00:10:58,230 --> 00:10:59,518
Lassie at seven.

316
00:10:59,698 --> 00:11:01,968
Then The Ed Sullivan Show at eight,

317
00:11:01,968 --> 00:11:04,563
which the whole family watched together.

318
00:11:04,743 --> 00:11:07,330
Ed Sullivan featured jugglers,

319
00:11:07,330 --> 00:11:09,486
comedians, opera singers,

320
00:11:09,486 --> 00:11:12,245
puppets and rock and roll stars,

321
00:11:12,245 --> 00:11:13,625
all in one hour.

322
00:11:13,805 --> 00:11:16,371
In September of nineteen fifty-six,

323
00:11:16,371 --> 00:11:20,037
about sixty million people watched Elvis Presley's

324
00:11:20,037 --> 00:11:22,164
first appearance on his show.

325
00:11:22,514 --> 00:11:25,719
After that, maybe Alfred Hitchcock Presents,

326
00:11:25,719 --> 00:11:27,030
or What's My Line?

327
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:29,393
And then, the dreaded moment.

328
00:11:29,573 --> 00:11:32,666
Bedtime, and the realization that tomorrow was

329
00:11:32,666 --> 00:11:33,137
Monday.

330
00:11:33,317 --> 00:11:35,267
Homework that hadn't been done.

331
00:11:35,447 --> 00:11:37,456
School clothes laid out.

332
00:11:37,636 --> 00:11:40,260
Fathers polishing their shoes for work.

333
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:41,879
Sunday was over.

334
00:11:42,230 --> 00:11:44,097
So what happened to that kind of

335
00:11:44,097 --> 00:11:44,505
Sunday?

336
00:11:44,685 --> 00:11:46,392
Blue laws faded away.

337
00:11:46,572 --> 00:11:47,779
Stores opened.

338
00:11:47,959 --> 00:11:49,190
Malls opened.

339
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:51,065
Families got busier.

340
00:11:51,245 --> 00:11:52,963
Both parents started working.

341
00:11:53,143 --> 00:11:56,208
Church attendance declined over the decades.

342
00:11:56,388 --> 00:11:59,674
Sports, shopping and errands filled up the

343
00:11:59,674 --> 00:11:59,987
day.

344
00:12:00,167 --> 00:12:03,395
And slowly, Sunday became just another day.

345
00:12:03,745 --> 00:12:06,527
Many people today say they miss the

346
00:12:06,527 --> 00:12:07,402
old Sunday.

347
00:12:07,582 --> 00:12:10,386
Not necessarily the scratchy clothes or the

348
00:12:10,386 --> 00:12:12,604
strict rules, but the feeling of a

349
00:12:12,604 --> 00:12:13,387
day of rest.

350
00:12:13,567 --> 00:12:15,876
A day when the whole town slowed

351
00:12:15,876 --> 00:12:16,237
down.

352
00:12:16,417 --> 00:12:18,693
When nobody expected you to work.

353
00:12:18,873 --> 00:12:21,060
When the family sat at the same

354
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:23,459
table, and had nowhere else to be.

355
00:12:23,809 --> 00:12:25,968
If you grew up in the fifties,

356
00:12:25,968 --> 00:12:27,408
you remember Sunday.

357
00:12:27,588 --> 00:12:28,737
The church bells.

358
00:12:28,917 --> 00:12:30,299
The starched dress.

359
00:12:30,479 --> 00:12:32,185
The smell of pot roast.

360
00:12:32,365 --> 00:12:33,944
The drive in the country.

361
00:12:34,124 --> 00:12:35,262
Ed Sullivan.

362
00:12:35,442 --> 00:12:38,137
And that strange quiet over the whole

363
00:12:38,137 --> 00:12:40,613
town, as if the world had decided,

364
00:12:40,613 --> 00:12:41,851
just for one day,

365
00:12:41,851 --> 00:12:43,453
to take a deep breath.

366
00:12:43,803 --> 00:12:45,660
So tell us in the comments.

367
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:47,587
What did Sunday look like in your

368
00:12:47,587 --> 00:12:48,487
house growing up?

369
00:12:48,667 --> 00:12:50,668
What was always on the table for

370
00:12:50,668 --> 00:12:53,106
Sunday dinner, and what did your family

371
00:12:53,106 --> 00:12:54,356
do in the afternoon?

372
00:12:54,536 --> 00:12:56,080
We read every comment.

373
00:12:56,260 --> 00:12:57,358
And thank you,

374
00:12:57,358 --> 00:12:58,534
from all of us,

375
00:12:58,534 --> 00:13:01,749
for spending these last fifteen days with

376
00:13:01,749 --> 00:13:01,984
us.

377
00:13:02,164 --> 00:13:03,557
There's much more to come.
